 ### Near-plane Culling
-A culling method using axis-aligned bounding boxes (AABB) to check depth and remove Gaussian points behind the camera near-plane [S11]. It filters out Gaussian points that lie behind or too close to the near clipping plane before they enter the rendering pipeline [S11]. It checks each Gaussian's position relative to the camera near plane, discarding or skipping points that fail the test before projection [S11]. The culling condition is $z_{\max} < z_{\text{near}}$, where $z$ is the Gaussian center depth in camera coordinates, $\Delta z \ge 0$ is the half-extent of the AABB along the camera z-axis, $z_{\min} = z - \Delta z$, $z_{\max} = z + \Delta z$, and $z_{\text{near}}$ is the camera near-plane depth [S11]. Hardware unit design operates with a single MAC unit, latency of 4 cycles, processing inputs $(x, y, \text{depth})$ and view matrix row $[3:0]$, using D flip-flops (DFF) between operations for timing stability at 800 MHz FP16 [S11]. It achieves a 56% culling rate on the compressed model, saving 56% of unnecessary Gaussian point computations and memory accesses [S11]. It increases throughput from 15.81% to 35.94% (2.27× gain), raising FPS from 20.4 to 46.4 at 1080p [S11].
